  "/case-studies/fitmax-competitor-audit": `
<article>
  <h1>Competitor Audit Case Study (Illustrative)</h1>
  <p><strong>Illustrative case study — not a specific client.</strong> By AAJ.</p>
  <p>Sample profile: B2C subscription fitness app.</p>
  <h2>Background</h2>
  <p>A mock competitor funnel audit showing how AAJ would dissect a large competitor's public paid/social presence and turn gaps into prioritized fixes for your own funnel.</p>
  <h2>Results</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>5 High-Impact Gaps Identified</li>
    <li>4 Priority Fixes</li>
    <li>90-Day Action Plan</li>
  </ul>
  <p><a href="${BASE}/case-studies">← All Case Studies</a></p>

  <h2>Phase 1 — Diagnose</h2>
  <p>The first step in every AAJ engagement is to replace assumptions with evidence. For an engagement of this shape, the Diagnose phase typically runs two to three weeks and includes a structured funnel audit, three to five customer interviews, a competitor and category scan, an analytics and tracking review, and a positioning and messaging assessment. The goal is to produce a written diagnosis that names the two or three constraints most responsible for the current outcome — not a 60-slide deck.</p>
  <p>In this engagement, the diagnosis surfaced clear patterns: messaging that did not match how buyers described the problem, a funnel that lost most volume between two specific stages, and a measurement system that could not attribute outcomes to channels with confidence. Each finding was tied to a quantifiable cost so leadership could see the size of the opportunity before approving any new spend.</p>

  <h2>Phase 2 — Design</h2>
  <p>The Design phase translated the diagnosis into a focused 60 to 90 day plan. AAJ does not produce omnibus strategies. The plan named three priorities, sequenced them by leverage and dependency, and assigned a single owner to each. Each priority had a measurable success criterion, a defined budget envelope, and a checkpoint date. Anything that did not fit those three priorities was explicitly deferred to the next quarter — not abandoned, but parked with a documented rationale.</p>
  <p>Design also produced the supporting assets the execution phase would depend on: a tested positioning statement, an updated landing page brief, a creative concept system, a measurement schema, and the dashboard wireframe. Producing these assets in the Design phase — rather than discovering they were missing mid-execution — was the difference between a smooth launch and a stalled one.</p>

  <h2>Phase 3 — Execute</h2>
  <p>The Execute phase ran the work. AAJ held the operating rhythm: weekly check-ins on leading indicators, biweekly reviews of lagging indicators, and a monthly steering session with leadership. The in-house team and external partners produced the deliverables; AAJ kept the system honest by surfacing slippage early, killing experiments that were not learning fast enough, and re-prioritizing capacity when a channel showed disproportionate lift.</p>
  <p>The results listed above were not produced by a single tactic. They were the compound effect of clearer positioning, a tighter funnel, a working measurement system, and a disciplined operating rhythm. That is the pattern that repeats across AAJ engagements regardless of category.</p>

  <h2>What other teams can learn from this</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Diagnose before designing. Most plateaus are caused by two or three constraints, not by a missing channel.</li>
    <li>Sequence the work. A plan with three named priorities outperforms a plan with twelve every time.</li>
    <li>Instrument first. A measurement system that lags execution by one quarter is a measurement system that does not exist.</li>
    <li>Hold the rhythm. The operating cadence — weekly, biweekly, monthly — is the mechanism that turns a strategy into pipeline.</li>
    <li>Kill experiments quickly. Speed of learning compounds; tolerance for slow experiments does not.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Related playbooks and case studies</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="${BASE}/playbooks/foundations-playbook">Foundations Playbook</a> — the positioning and ICP work underneath this engagement.</li>
    <li><a href="${BASE}/playbooks/analytics-playbook">Analytics &amp; Attribution Playbook</a> — the measurement system that made the results visible.</li>
    <li><a href="${BASE}/playbooks/90-Day-Growth-Playbook">90-Day Growth Playbook</a> — the compressed sprint format used to operationalize the plan.</li>
    <li><a href="${BASE}/case-studies">All AAJ case studies</a></li>
    <li><a href="${BASE}/contact">Talk to AAJ</a> about a similar engagement.</li>
  </ul>
  <section aria-labelledby="faq-heading">
    <h2 id="faq-heading">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <div>
      <h3>What is a competitor audit?</h3>
      <p>A structured analysis of 5–10 competitors covering positioning, pricing, features, content strategy, and market gaps — used to inform strategic decisions.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h3>How did the audit help this illustrative app profile?</h3>
      <p>The audit revealed underserved market segments and positioning gaps that this illustrative app profile could own, leading to a focused GTM strategy and accelerated growth.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h3>How long does a competitor audit take?</h3>
      <p>AAJ's standard competitor audit runs 2–3 weeks and delivers a written report, positioning recommendations, and a prioritized roadmap of opportunities.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</article>`,

